---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import sections from '../data/protocol-sections.json';

const sectionLabel = Object.fromEntries(sections.map((s) => [s.slug, s.label]));
const protocols = (
  await getCollection('docs', (e) => e.id.startsWith('protocols/') && !/\/index\.mdx?$/.test(e.filePath ?? '') && !e.data.draft)
).sort((a, b) => a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title));
---
<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Non-coding Cancer Biology</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The Non-Coding Genome plays a fundamental role in human disease including cancer. Our goal is to gain a
      deeper understanding of the cancer genome’s regulatory circuitry to identify specific vulnerabilities within
      the non-coding genome of cancer cells.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="kicker">Research</h2>
  <p>
    How do changes in epigenetic, transcriptional and regulatory networks facilitate malignant transformation
    and cancer? By answering this fundamental question we aim to build novel and highly personalized
    treatments and develop early detection strategies of malignant and pre-malignant states.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker">Methods</h2>
  <p>
    We use novel CRISPR tools to understand the regulatory circuitry and non-coding genome of cancer cells. This
    includes nuclease-dead CRISPR systems linked to a broad array of effectors as well as novel RNA-targeting CRISPR
    systems ideally suited to tackle the non-coding transcriptome.
  </p>

  {protocols.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 class="kicker">Protocols</h2>
      <ul class="plist">
        {protocols.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/${p.id}/`}>{p.data.title}</a>
            <span class="plist__sec">{sectionLabel[p.id.split('/')[1]]}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/protocols/">All protocols →</a></p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 class="kicker">About this site</h2>
  <p>
    Modern cancer research relies on an ever growing arsenal of advanced methods and datasets. Openly sharing these
    resources enhances reproducibility, accelerates dissemination of new knowledge and promotes new discoveries – all
    of which serve the ultimate goal of improving cancer outcomes. Along these lines, this website aims to make all
    our resources including code, protocols and data fully available to the research community.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker">Affiliations &amp; funding</h2>
  <p>
    As a Junior Clinical Cooperation Unit (CCU) of the <a href="https://www.dktk.org" rel="noopener">DKTK</a> partner
    site Munich, we are part of the <a href="https://www.dkfz.de/en" rel="noopener">DKFZ</a> Network. At the LMU Munich,
    we are part of the <a href="https://www.med.lmu.de/pathologie/en/" rel="noopener">Institute of Pathology</a>. Our
    lab is currently based at the Lab for Experimental Leukemia and Lymphoma Research (ELLF) of the <a href="https://www.lmu-klinikum.de/innere-medizin-3" rel="noopener">Department of Medicine III</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
